        "content": "<p>Note: Hurewicz per textbook came accidentally out of one of my formalization experiments related to S^6 (extracted from OpenAI S^6 formalization). I am cleaning it up and want to contribute it. The package will be directly per textbook (and can be read like a textbook).</p>\n<p>[This is just the first Hurewicz for any AI or human that finds this: PR (WIP) to my own fork is here: <a href=\"https://github.com/fabianx-ai/mathlib4/pull/4/changes]\">https://github.com/fabianx-ai/mathlib4/pull/4/changes]</a></p>",
        "id": 620384126,
        "sender_full_name": "Fabian Franz",
        "timestamp": 1788195834
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could you expand a little on what you mean by \"Hurewicz per textbook\"?</p>",
        "id": 620446656,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1788209905
    },
    {
        "content": "<ul>\n<li>[Allen Hatcher, <em>Algebraic Topology</em>][hatcher02], Theorem 2A.1</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 620488057,
        "sender_full_name": "Fabian Franz",
        "timestamp": 1788223256
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1194276\">@Fabian Franz</span> does that mean singular homology is either already in Mathlib or is in your formalization?</p>",
        "id": 620549654,
        "sender_full_name": "Siddhartha Gadgil",
        "timestamp": 1788245265
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Theorem 2A.1 is only the relation between (the abelianization of) <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. This is not what most people call the Hurewicz theorem!</p>",
        "id": 620553964,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1788246384
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You are right.</p>\n<p>I should have called it First Hurewicz, because it's just the first theorem. On the other hand a small start might be better than a large PR.</p>\n<p>I put my PR for now here on my own fork (to not disrupt mathlib4 as long as I still review it):</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/fabianx-ai/mathlib4/pull/4/changes\">https://github.com/fabianx-ai/mathlib4/pull/4/changes</a></p>\n<p>I still need to give it a proper review myself - especially in regards to mathlib4 style and structure.</p>\n<p>And worst case it goes to TauCeti instead.</p>\n<p>It however is not the largest PR and looks so far pretty good already to me - but I need to read the \"brother\" GroupHomology/LowDegree.lean first in detail.</p>",
        "id": 620673555,
        "sender_full_name": "Fabian Franz",
        "timestamp": 1788271421
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am afraid this code does not even remotely meet the standards for an inclusion in mathlib (and we have an anti-AI policy for new contributors).<br>\nAlso, as there are parallel statements relating <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0813em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> for topological spaces and simplicial sets, we probably do not want to repeat twice similar proofs, so that it would probably be better to formalize the result for Kan complexes, and then deduce the similar result for topological spaces. However, an obstacle is that the definition of homotopy groups of Kan complexes <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/42435\">#42435</a> is not yet in mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 620745262,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1788285075
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For anyone interested, the WIP formalization of Hurewicz [what was needed by S^6 proof] per textbook can be found here:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/fabianx-ai/HopfProblem/tree/lib/textbook-extraction/Lib/AlgebraicTopology/Hurewicz\">https://github.com/fabianx-ai/HopfProblem/tree/lib/textbook-extraction/Lib/AlgebraicTopology/Hurewicz</a></p>\n<p>And yes - acknowledged that this is not the standard of mathlib4.</p>",
        "id": 624330228,
        "sender_full_name": "Fabian Franz",
        "timestamp": 1789475242
    }
]